📦 SECTION 1: Linux Fundamentals (Fresher Level)
❓ Q1. What is Linux?
✅ How to Answer:

"Linux is a free, open-source, Unix-like operating system kernel created by Linus Torvalds in 1991. It's widely used in servers, cloud platforms, embedded systems, and supercomputers. A complete OS = Linux kernel + GNU tools + a package manager = called a Linux Distribution (distro)."

Popular Distros to mention: Ubuntu, CentOS, RHEL, Debian, Fedora, Arch Linux

💡 Interview Tip: Always differentiate between Linux (the kernel) and a Linux Distribution (the full OS).

❓ Q2. What is the difference between Linux and Unix?
Feature	Linux	Unix
Cost	Free & Open Source	Mostly Paid/Proprietary
Source Code	Publicly available	Closed source
Usage	Servers, Cloud, Desktops	HP-UX, Solaris, AIX
Portability	Highly portable	Less portable
Community	Large open-source community	Vendor-specific support
✅ Model Answer: "Linux is open-source and free, derived from Unix principles but rewritten from scratch. Unix is a proprietary OS. Linux is more portable and community-driven, while Unix runs on specific hardware platforms."

❓ Q3. What is the Linux Kernel?
✅ Model Answer:

"The kernel is the core of the operating system that manages communication between hardware and software. It handles four key areas:

Process Management – creating/scheduling processes
Memory Management – allocating RAM
Device Management – communicating with hardware via drivers
File System Management – reading/writing files"
Command to check kernel version:

Copyuname -r          # Shows kernel version
uname -a          # Full system information
cat /etc/os-release  # OS details and distro name
❓ Q4. What is BASH?
✅ Model Answer:

"BASH stands for Bourne Again Shell. It's the default command-line interpreter in most Linux distributions. It processes commands entered by the user, executes scripts, and supports features like variables, loops, conditionals, and functions."

Other shells to mention: zsh, ksh, csh, tcsh, fish

❓ Q5. What is Swap Space?
✅ Model Answer:

"Swap space is a portion of the hard disk used as virtual RAM when physical memory (RAM) is exhausted. It's slower than RAM but prevents the system from crashing due to out-of-memory errors. The standard recommendation is 2x the RAM size (for systems with <2GB RAM) or equal to RAM for larger systems."

Copyfree -h        # Check swap usage
swapon --show  # Show active swap
