❓ Q7. What are File Permissions in Linux?
✅ Model Answer:

"Linux uses a three-level permission model: Owner, Group, Others. Each level has Read (r=4), Write (w=2), Execute (x=1) permissions."

Copyls -l file.txt
# Output: -rwxr-xr-- 1 user group 1234 Jan 1 file.txt
#          ^ ^ ^
#          | | └─ Others: r-- = 4 (read only)
#          | └─── Group:  r-x = 5 (read+execute)
#          └───── Owner:  rwx = 7 (full access)

chmod 755 file.txt     # Set permissions numerically
chmod +x script.sh     # Add execute permission
chown user:group file  # Change ownership
💡 Explain the octal system (rwx = 4+2+1=7) — this always impresses interviewers.

❓ Q8. What is the difference between Hard Link and Soft Link?
Feature	Hard Link	Soft Link (Symlink)
Command	ln file link	ln -s file link
Points to	Same inode	File path/name
Works across filesystems?	❌ No	✅ Yes
If original deleted?	File still accessible	Link breaks
Works for directories?	❌ No	✅ Yes
Copyln file.txt hardlink.txt        # Hard link
ln -s file.txt softlink.txt     # Soft (symbolic) link
❓ Q9. What is an Inode?
✅ Model Answer:

"An inode is a data structure that stores metadata about a file — including permissions, ownership, timestamps, and pointers to data blocks — but NOT the filename. Every file has a unique inode number."

Copyls -i file.txt    # Show inode number
stat file.txt     # Detailed inode info
